@@ -31,3 +31,16 @@ It is *highly* inadvisable to generate production apps while this environment va
 Both macOS and Windows provide mechanisms for running x86_64 binaries on ARM64 hardware. These tools can make it very difficult to generate a packaged application, as it can be difficult to ensure a packaged application contains code matching the right architecture when the platform provides misleading answers about the current platform. As a result, Briefcase will raise an error if it detects that the version of Python that is being used doesn't match the underlying hardware architecture.
 
 However, it is possible to disable this check by setting the `BRIEFCASE_ALLOW_EMULATION` environment variable. This will cause Briefcase to output a warning if it appears that Python is running in emulation mode, but it will allow Briefcase commands to run.
+
+### HTTP configuration
+
+Briefcase uses `httpx2` to perform HTTP requests. `httpx2` honors six environment variables, which can be used to configure the certificates and proxies used when downloading support packages and tools:
+
+- `SSL_CERT_FILE` - The path to a file containing trusted CA certificates. If set, this replaces the system certificate store.
+- `SSL_CERT_DIR` - The path to a directory containing trusted CA certificates. If set, this replaces the system certificate store.
+- `HTTP_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy to use for HTTP requests.
+- `HTTPS_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy to use for HTTPS requests.
+- `ALL_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy to use for all requests when a protocol-specific proxy isn't configured.
+- `NO_PROXY` - A comma-separated list of hostnames, domains, IP addresses, or CIDR ranges for which a proxy should not be used.
+
+For full details on how these variables are interpreted, including the supported proxy URL schemes and the format of `NO_PROXY`, see the [httpx2 environment variable documentation](https://pydantic.dev/docs/httpx2/api/environment_variables/).
